{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KqV26gXJUW7NeF8wUdudx6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Idea Brief: When Do LLM Annotation Errors Matter? Multi-Class Bias Expressions and Annotation Sensitivity Values for Downstream Social Science Inference\n2\t\n3\t\n4\t- **Idea ID**: `idea_027`\n5\t- **Created**: 2026-05-20\n6\t- **Approved**: 2026-05-20\n7\t- **Source**: foreign:be-idea-gen-0515-4\n8\t- **Level**: solid\n9\t- **Target Venues**: ARR October 2026 (deadline: ~2026-10-15; commit to NAACL 2027 or later *ACL venues), AAAI 2027 (abstract: 2026-07-21, paper: 2026-07-28), ICWSM 2027 Round 2 (deadline: 2026-09-15)\n10\t\n11\t## Seed Topic\n12\t\n13\tweb_research\n14\t\n15\t## Generation Journey\n16\t\n17\t1. **Start** — 开始生成，seed: '## Seed 4: Open-Vocabulary Affordance Zero-Shot VLA\\n\\nObjective: CoRL 2026 submission.\\n\\nDirection: Traditional affordance methods and VLAs rely on fixed category annotations and struggle with open-world unknown objects and free-form interaction instructions. CoA-VLA (arXiv 2412.20451, December 2024) integrates affordance chain-of-reasoning into VLA, but uses 4 predefined structured affordance types (object, grasp, spatial, motion) — not truly open-vocabulary — and cannot handle free-form interaction descriptions like \"use this thing to wipe off the dust over there.\" GeneralVLA (arXiv 2602.04315, February 2026) achieves zero-shot affordance perception and manipulation trajectory generation through VLM+SAM, but is a modular three-stage pipeline (affordance perception → trajectory planning → low-level control), not a unified end-to-end VLA — with information loss between modules and inability to jointly optimize. OVAL-Prompt (ICRA 2024 VLMNM Workshop, arXiv 2404.11000) achieves truly open-vocabulary affordance localization, but only performs detection/localization without generating actions. AffordDP (CVPR 2025, arXiv 2412.03142) achieves affordance cross-category transfer through 3D contact points and trajectories, but completely lacks language conditioning. Core gap: No work unifies open-vocabulary affordance reasoning into an end-to-end VLA for zero-shot manipulation — existing work either has fixed affordance types (CoA-VLA), or is a modular pipeline (GeneralVLA), or only does detection without actions (OVAL-Prompt), or lacks language (AffordDP).\\n\\nSeed Papers:\\n- \"OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\" (Kim et al., CoRL 2024, arXiv 2406.09246)\\n  — 7B parameter open-source VLA, trained on 970K real robot demonstrations, establishing the VLA open-source paradigm. The base architecture for this work — integrating open-vocabulary affordance reasoning on top.\\n- \"CoA-VLA: Improving Vision-Language-Action Models via Visual-Textual Chain-of-Affordance\" (Li et al., arXiv 2412.20451, December 2024)\\n  — Integrates affordance chain-of-reasoning into VLA: model sequentially reasons about object affordance, grasp affordance, spatial affordance, and motion affordance before predicting actions. Uses visual-language collaborative injection module. Surpasses OpenVLA and Octo. Key limitation: 4 affordance types are predefined/structured, not open-vocabulary — cannot handle arbitrary natural language descriptions of novel interactions.\\n- \"GeneralVLA: Generalizable Vision-Language-Action Models with Knowledge-Guided Trajectory Planning\" (Ma et al., arXiv 2602.04315, February 2026)\\n  — Hierarchical three-module pipeline: (1) VLM+SAM affordance perception for keypoint detection, (2) mid-level planning agent for trajectory generation, (3) low-level control policy. Achieves zero-shot manipulation across 14 tasks without real-world data. Key limitation: Modular pipeline, not end-to-end — affordance module, trajectory planner, and controller are separated, with information bottlenecks between modules.\\n- \"OVAL-Prompt: Open-Vocabulary Affordance Localization for Robot Manipulation through LLM Affordance-Grounding\" (Tong et al., ICRA 2024 VLMNM Workshop, arXiv 2404.11000)\\n  — Prompt-based method combining VLM for object part segmentation and LLM for part-affordance grounding mapping, achieving open-vocabulary affordance localization without prior training. Competitive with supervised methods on localization benchmarks. Key limitation: Only performs affordance detection/localization, does not generate robot actions — a perception module, not a complete manipulation system.\\n- \"AffordDP: Generalizable Diffusion Policy with Transferable Affordance\" (Wu et al., CVPR 2025, arXiv 2412.03142)\\n  — Models manipulation priors through 3D contact points and post-contact trajectories, achieving affordance cross-category transfer using visual models + point cloud registration. Affordance-guided diffusion policy generates actions. Key limitation: No language conditioning — affordances are geometric/contact-based, not language-driven, cannot handle open-vocabulary instructions.\\n\\nKnown Gap:\\n- CoA-VLA integrates affordance into VLA but uses predefined structured types, does not support open vocabulary\\n- GeneralVLA does zero-shot affordance manipulation but is a modular pipeline, not end-to-end\\n- OVAL-Prompt does open-vocabulary affordance but only detection, no action generation\\n- AffordDP does affordance cross-category transfer but no language conditioning\\n- VoxPoser (CoRL 2023, arXiv 2307.05973) uses LLM to generate 3D affordance/constraint maps but is modular, no end-to-end learning\\n- No work unifies open-vocabulary affordance reasoning into end-to-end VLA\\n\\nIn Scope: Methods unifying open-vocabulary affordance reasoning into end-to-end VLA architecture. Specifically includes: text prompt-driven open-vocabulary affordance feature activation mechanism, universal shape-function-interaction attribute association learning, end-to-end joint training of affordance reasoning and action generation, zero-shot generalization to unseen object categories and novel language interaction instructions. The core contribution should be a unified architecture, not a modular concatenation of affordance detection + action generation.\\n\\nOut of Scope: Predefined fixed affordance types (CoA-VLA already done). Modular affordance pipelines (GeneralVLA/VoxPoser already done). Affordance detection only without actions (OVAL-Prompt already done). Affordance transfer without language conditioning (AffordDP already done). Pure affordance benchmarking/analysis.\\n\\nContribution Type: Method contribution. Output must include (1) unified architecture design for open-vocabulary affordance and VLA, (2) end-to-end training strategy for affordance reasoning and action generation, (3) experimental validation of zero-shot generalization on unseen objects and novel interaction instructions, demonstrating advantages over structured affordance VLA and modular methods.\\n\\nEvaluation Protocol:\\nTraining: Based on OpenVLA architecture, integrating open-vocabulary affordance module. Training data uses LIBERO + affordance annotation data (e.g., AGD20K/UMD Affordance).\\nEvaluation Metrics:\\n- Primary metric: Zero-shot manipulation success rate on unseen object categories\\n- Novel interaction instruction generalization: Language interaction descriptions unseen during training (e.g., train on \"pick up,\" test on \"scoop out\")\\n- Affordance localization precision: Compare with detection methods like OVAL-Prompt (ensure affordance understanding itself does not degrade)\\n- Manipulation success rate: Compare with standard VLA baselines (ensure adding affordance does not degrade known task performance)\\n- Baseline comparisons:\\n  1. Affordance-free VLA (OpenVLA direct fine-tuning)\\n  2. Structured affordance VLA (CoA-VLA reproduction)\\n  3. Modular affordance pipeline (GeneralVLA approach)\\n  4. Affordance detection + independent policy (OVAL-Prompt + policy learning)\\n  5. LLM affordance map + planning (VoxPoser approach)\\n- Ablation studies: Open-vocabulary affordance vs. fixed-type affordance, end-to-end vs. staged training, text-driven affordance vs. vision-driven affordance\\n- Cross-object transfer tests: Shape-function association generalization (e.g., if trained on shovel\\'s \"scooping\" affordance, can the model activate the same interaction for novel shovel-like tools?)\\n- Free-form instruction tests: Non-templated natural language instruction understanding\\n\\nBuilding Blocks: OpenVLA codebase (VLA base architecture); CoA-VLA codebase (affordance VLA baseline); OVAL-Prompt codebase (open-vocabulary affordance detection reference); AGD20K/UMD Affordance datasets (affordance annotations); LIBERO benchmark (manipulation task evaluation); DINOv2/SigLIP visual encoders; SAM2 (segmentation); HuggingFace transformers + accelerate; CLIP/SigLIP (open-vocabulary semantic alignment).\\n\\nHard Constraints:\\nCompute: ≤200 H100-hours. Strategy: Fine-tune based on 7B VLA, affordance module leveraging pre-trained VLM features (e.g., CLIP/SigLIP) to reduce training requirements. Training ~80 H100-hours, baseline reproduction ~50 H100-hours, ablation ~40 H100-hours, generalization testing ~30 H100-hours.\\nEvaluation: Must validate zero-shot generalization on unseen object categories. Must include free-form language instruction tests. Must include ≥4 baselines. Must report both affordance precision and manipulation success rate. Must include ablation studies and cross-object transfer tests.\\n\\nExploration Directions: (1) End-to-end activation of VLM internal affordance features: Rather than adding an external affordance detection module to VLA, activate interaction-relevant visual features within VLA\\'s VLM backbone network directly through language instructions. Key insight: Large-scale VLMs (e.g., PaliGemma, SigLIP) have already learned extensive shape-function associations during pre-training (\"knives can cut,\" \"bowls can hold\"), only requiring suitable activation mechanisms to release this implicit knowledge. (2) Affordance as intermediate reasoning tokens: Rather than explicitly outputting affordance heatmaps, have VLA generate a set of \"affordance reasoning tokens\" before generating action token sequences (similar to CoA-VLA\\'s chain reasoning but open-vocabulary). These tokens encode interaction possibilities of objects in the current scene, then condition subsequent action generation. Differs from CoA-VLA: not limited to 4 predefined types; reasoning content is dynamically determined by language instructions. (3) Contrastive learning for universal shape-function-interaction mapping: Add contrastive objective during VLA training to pull together different-shaped objects with similar functions (various \"tools that can scoop things\") in affordance feature space, and push apart similar-shaped objects with different functions (decorative bowl vs. functional bowl). More flexible than CoA-VLA\\'s structured types — any natural language-described interaction can participate in contrasting. (4) Knowledge distillation for zero-shot affordance reasoning: Use large VLMs\\' (e.g., GPT-4V level) zero-shot affordance reasoning capability (\"how can this object be interacted with?\") as teacher signals, distilling into small VLA. Large models have rich commonsense knowledge and can generate affordance descriptions for virtually any object, but are too large to deploy directly on robots. (5) Affordance consistency self-supervision: The same object under different language descriptions should have consistent affordance responses (\"grab the handle\" vs. \"pick it up by the stick part\" describe the same interaction). Use this consistency as self-supervision signals, reducing dependency on fine-grained affordance annotations.\\n\\nAvoid:\\n- Using predefined fixed affordance types — duplicates CoA-VLA\\n- Modular pipeline (affordance detection → planning → control) — duplicates GeneralVLA/VoxPoser, and prevents end-to-end optimization\\n- Only doing affordance detection without actions — duplicates OVAL-Prompt\\n- Ignoring CoA-VLA baseline — it is the most recent affordance + VLA work; must directly compare\\n- Ignoring GeneralVLA — it is the most recent zero-shot affordance manipulation work; must clarify advantages of end-to-end vs. modular\\n- Reporting results only on within-training-distribution objects — the core value of open vocabulary is on out-of-distribution objects\\n- Over-relying on affordance annotation data — should design methods to reduce annotation requirements\\n\\nIdea Generation Quality Constraints (Hard rules based on historical cases):\\n\\n1. No strawman problems: Any claim of \"existing methods assume X\" or \"existing methods fail on Y\" must cite specific paragraphs from specific papers as evidence.\\n\\n2. No pure observation ideas: Output must include a complete method, not merely analysis/benchmark/empirical study.\\n\\n3. No mediocre methods: Methods must answer:\\n   - Core insight: Large-scale VLMs have already implicitly learned rich shape-function association knowledge during pre-training; CoA-VLA released part of it through structured reasoning chains, but is limited by predefined types. We directly activate corresponding affordance features within VLA through open-vocabulary language instructions, fully releasing VLM\\'s implicit shape-function knowledge into action generation.\\n   - Why non-obvious: (1) CoA-VLA (December 2024) is the first work integrating affordance chain reasoning into VLA; affordance + VLA itself is a new paradigm; (2) open-vocabulary requires solving the infinite-dimensional affordance space problem — unlike 4 fixed types that can be directly classified; (3) end-to-end training of open-vocabulary affordance requires affordance annotation data, but such data is scarce — needs training strategies that reduce annotation dependency.\\n   - What information is exploited: Implicit shape-function association knowledge from VLM pre-training — which neither 2D visual VLAs nor fixed-type affordance methods fully utilize.\\n   - Mechanistic argument: When given a free-form language instruction (e.g., \"scoop the rice\"), the VLM internally already encodes knowledge that \"scooping requires a concave container-shaped tool.\" Through suitable activation mechanisms, this knowledge can be transformed into affordance attention in visual feature space — focusing on regions in the scene with corresponding geometric functional properties, then conditioning action generation. More flexible than CoA-VLA\\'s fixed 4-type reasoning because affordance is dynamically defined by language.\\n\\n4. No duplicating existing work: Each core technical component must be confirmed with at least 3 different search queries to have no directly related work.\\n\\n5. No padding experiments:\\n   - \"Delete experiment test\": Keep only the method description — does it teach the reader something non-trivial? Yes — VLM implicit shape-function knowledge can drive VLA action generation through open-vocabulary activation.\\n   - \"Baseline fear test\": If CoA-VLA\\'s 4 structured types already cover most scenarios, is the paper still valuable? Must prove open-vocabulary methods\\' advantages on free-form instructions and novel interactions.\\n   - \"Citation test\": Will people cite the numbers or the insight 5 years later? The insight — open-vocabulary affordance in end-to-end VLA paradigm.\\n\\n6. The following method patterns are rejected (unless with extremely strong structural reasons):\\n   - \"Add module X\" — Why this specific structure?\\n   - \"Train on data Y\" — Is data construction itself a contribution?\\n   - \"Transfer Z from domain A to domain B\" — What is the structural analogy?\\n   - \"Combine P and Q\" — Where is the synergy? Combining open-vocabulary detection (OVAL-Prompt) with VLA — must prove end-to-end joint training outperforms simple concatenation.\\n   - \"Use more/larger/stronger X\" — Scale is not a method contribution.\\n\\nCompetition Warning: CoA-VLA (December 2024) is the most recent affordance + VLA — uses structured reasoning chains but not open-vocabulary. GeneralVLA (February 2026) does zero-shot affordance manipulation but is a modular pipeline. OVAL-Prompt (ICRA 2024 Workshop) does open-vocabulary affordance detection but no actions. AffordDP (CVPR 2025) does affordance cross-category transfer but no language. VoxPoser (CoRL 2023) uses LLM to generate 3D affordance maps but is modular. π0 (Physical Intelligence) and RT-2 and other large VLAs may implicitly contain partial affordance knowledge but do not explicitly leverage it. Differentiation core: The only work integrating open-vocabulary affordance reasoning end-to-end into a unified VLA architecture. Highest risk: CoA-VLA is closest in space; if its author team quickly extends to open-vocabulary, differentiation shrinks. Monitor: CoA-VLA author team, GeneralVLA author team, Physical Intelligence (whether π0 follow-ups will integrate affordance).\\n\\nTags: direction=open_vocab_affordance_zero_shot_vla, contribution_type=method, sub_direction=unified_affordance_vla_end_to_end, target_venues=[\"CoRL 2026\"].'\n18\t2. **Problem Discovery** (492s) — 发现 4 个研究方向\n19\t   - Open-Vocabulary Affordance Chain-of-Reasoning for End-to-End VLA\n20\t   - Activating Implicit VLM Affordance Knowledge for Manipulation\n21\t   - Affordance-Conditioned Flow Matching for Open-Vocabulary VLA\n22\t   - Functional Correspondence Learning for Zero-Shot Affordance Transfer\n23\t3. **Method Synthesis** (626s) — 完成: \"Open-Vocabulary Affordance Chain-of-Reasoning for End-to-End VLA\" [top-tier] → ICLR 2027 (abstract Sep 19, paper Sep 24, 2026) — top-tier ML venue, best fit for method contribution, AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28, 2026) — top-tier AI venue, earlier deadline, CoRL 2026 (abstract May 26, paper May 29, 2026) — top-tier robotics venue, tight but feasible with auto-research, CVPR 2027 (abstract Nov 7, paper Nov 13, 2026) — top-tier vision venue, fallback if earlier submissions need revision\n24\t3. **Method Synthesis** (813s) — 完成: \"Activating Implicit VLM Affordance Knowledge for Manipulation\" [top-tier] → ICLR 2027 (abstract Sep 19, paper Sep 24, 2026 — highest-tier ML venue, ideal for the VLM interpretability + VLA generalization contribution), CoRL 2026 (abstract May 26, paper May 29, 2026 — premier robotics learning venue, tight deadline but perfect topical fit), AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28, 2026 — broad top-tier AI venue, comfortable timeline)\n25\t3. **Method Synthesis** (4s) — 完成: \"Affordance-Conditioned Flow Matching for Open-Vocabulary VLA\"\n26\t3. **Method Synthesis** (4s) — 完成: \"Functional Correspondence Learning for Zero-Shot Affordance Transfer\"\n27\t\n28\t## Core Problem\n29\t\n30\tLLM annotation errors can bias downstream social science conclusions, but researchers currently lack tools to answer two critical questions: (1) which specific annotation error structures actually propagate bias under my study's causal design, and (2) how severe must differential annotation error be to overturn my conclusion? This is not a hypothetical concern: Baumann, Röttger et al. (2025) demonstrate empirically across 37 tasks from 21 studies that approximately 31% of downstream conclusions are incorrect when using SOTA LLM annotations, rising to 50% for smaller models. Existing causal frameworks for text classifier measurement error (Wood-Doughty et al. 2018; TeBlunthuis et al. 2024) address the first question only for binary classification in limited DAG topologies, while sensitivity analysis tools analogous to E-values (VanderWeele & Ding 2017) or measurement error bounds (VanderWeele & Li 2019) do not exist for annotation-induced bias. The correction methods available (DSL, PPI, MLA) each assume specific error structures, but without diagnostic tools to characterize error structure, researchers cannot determine which correction is appropriate — or whether correction is needed at all. Meanwhile, the epidemiological measurement-error literature (Hernán & Cole 2009; VanderWeele & Hernán 2012) provides the causal foundations but has not been applied to the multi-class LLM annotation setting where confusion matrices replace simple misclassification rates.\n31\t\n32\t## Opportunity / Why Now\n33\t\n34\tTwo converging gaps create a focused opportunity. First, the diagnostic gap: Wood-Doughty et al. (2018) produce no formal bias theorems and defer identifiability conditions, and TeBlunthuis et al. (2024) provide correction only for binary outcomes with a single classifier — yet most social science annotation tasks involve multi-class labels (protest type, stance category, topic), and bias propagation in the multi-class case is qualitatively different because directional bias depends on which categories are confused, not just overall error rates. The epidemiological literature confirms this complexity: Brenner (1993) shows that non-differential misclassification of polytomous confounders can bias in either direction, and Veierød & Laake (2001) prove that multi-category exposure misclassification produces bias that depends on all true category-specific coefficients weighted by the full misclassification probability matrix. No systematic treatment exists for how K-class confusion matrix structures map to downstream bias across different causal designs in the text annotation context. Second, the sensitivity gap: no sensitivity analysis framework exists for annotation-induced bias — researchers cannot report 'how differential must annotation error be to overturn my conclusion?' VanderWeele & Li (2019) provide sensitivity bounds for differential measurement error, but only for binary exposure/outcome settings using risk ratios, and explicitly do not address polytomous extensions or confusion-matrix parameterizations. Bridging these two gaps — extending bias expressions to multi-class annotation and providing annotation-specific sensitivity analysis — would give social science researchers actionable diagnostic tools to evaluate the robustness of LLM-annotated studies, complementing existing correction methods rather than replacing them. The urgency is underscored by Baumann et al. (2025), who show that 31% of downstream conclusions from LLM annotations are already incorrect — researchers need diagnostic tools now.\n35\t\n36\t## Landscape (SOTA & Limitations)\n37\t\n38\tThe causal treatment of measurement error in DAGs is well-established in epidemiology: Hernán & Cole (2009) formalize starred-node DAGs with differential/non-differential and independent/dependent error taxonomy; VanderWeele & Hernán (2012) prove bias-direction results using signed DAGs; Li et al. (2020) connect causal structure between true variables to the type of measurement error that arises; VanderWeele & Li (2019) provide sensitivity bounds for differential measurement error of binary exposure/outcome using risk ratios; Schuessler & Selb (SMR 2025) apply DAGs to survey measurement error. For polytomous variables specifically, Brenner (1993) analyzes non-differential misclassification of polytomous confounders showing bias can go in either direction, and Veierød & Laake (2001) derive bias expressions for multi-category exposure misclassification in Poisson regression, showing bias in each category depends on all true coefficients weighted by misclassification probabilities — but neither connects to DAG-based causal reasoning or sensitivity analysis. In NLP, Wood-Doughty, Shpitser & Dredze (EMNLP 2018) apply DAG reasoning to text classifier errors with matrix adjustment correction but produce no formal bias theorems, no identifiability conditions, and note severe instability in corrections. TeBlunthuis, Hase & Chan (CMM 2024) provide the most complete treatment: four DAG scenarios (differential/non-differential × IV/DV), testable conditions via likelihood ratio tests, and a Maximum Likelihood Adjustment (MLA) method — but only for binary classification with a single classifier, and without LLM-specific considerations. On the LLM annotation side, Baumann, Röttger et al. (2025, arXiv:2509.08825) provide the strongest empirical motivation for diagnostic tools: across 37 annotation tasks from 21 published social science studies and 13 million labels from 18 models, they find approximately 31% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with SOTA LLMs, rising to 50% for smaller models, coining the term 'LLM hacking' to describe how configuration choices can make 'virtually anything statistically significant.' Messing (April 2026, arXiv:2604.11581) decomposes LLM pipeline variance via linear mixed models, finding item-judge interaction dominates at 44%, but explicitly excludes causal identification. Gligorić et al. (NAACL 2025) note correlated errors bias estimates and propose DSL/PPI corrections. Jerzak et al. (EMNLP 2025) benchmark correction methods but treat error as monolithic. Halterman & Keith (arXiv 2025) show conceptualization-induced bias cannot be fixed by accuracy improvements alone. Chew et al. (arXiv 2026, 2604.07591) decompose human labeling variance but provide no causal-propagation framework. Cheng, Mayya & Sedoc (arXiv 2024, 2412.14461) decompose LLM annotation error into guideline-, baseline-, prompt-, and model-induced components via their SILICON framework but do not connect to causal propagation. Crucially, recent work on correlated LLM errors reveals that the assumption of independent errors across LLMs is violated in practice: Kim, Garg, Peng & Garg (ICML 2025, arXiv:2506.07962) show models agree 60% of the time when both err, with larger models having highly correlated errors even across architectures; Balasubramanian, Podkopaev & Kasiviswanathan (arXiv 2026, 2601.22336) formalize dependence-aware aggregation via Ising models, proving conditional-independence methods are strictly suboptimal; CARE (Zhao et al., arXiv 2026, 2603.00039) demonstrates confounder-aware aggregation with identifiability guarantees under shared confounders. Mehrotra, Visokay & Gligorić (arXiv 2026, 2603.21404) introduce perspective-driven inference for subjective annotation tasks. No existing work provides: (a) multi-class extension of bias expressions across canonical social-science DAG topologies, or (b) sensitivity analysis values for annotation-induced bias parameterized by confusion matrices.\n39\t\n40\t## Proposed Approach\n41\t\n42\tWe extend the causal measurement-error framework for text annotation in two directions, building explicitly on Wood-Doughty et al. (2018), TeBlunthuis et al. (2024), and the epidemiological sensitivity analysis tradition (VanderWeele & Li 2019), with connections to the polytomous misclassification literature (Brenner 1993; Veierød & Laake 2001). (1) MULTI-CLASS BIAS EXPRESSIONS IN MATRIX FORM: Model the LLM annotation process as a noisy measurement node A* within a structural causal model connecting true multi-class label A to covariates X, treatment T, and outcome Y, parameterizing the error mechanism via the full K×K confusion matrix C where C_{ji} = P(A*=j|A=i,X,T). Derive bias expressions for six canonical social-science DAG topologies (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias, instrument, front-door), proving formally which confusion matrix structures produce downstream-innocuous vs. downstream-toxic error for each topology. IMPORTANT QUALIFICATION ON TRACTABILITY: For K=2 (binary), our expressions reduce to the scalar formulas of TeBlunthuis et al. (2024), providing a consistency check. For K=3, we provide fully worked scalar closed-form expressions that practitioners can directly compute and interpret — this is the 'sweet spot' covering common annotation tasks like stance detection (pro/neutral/anti) and sentiment (positive/neutral/negative). For K≥5, bias expressions are necessarily in matrix notation involving confusion matrix algebra (products, inversions, spectral decompositions) rather than scalar closed-form, because a K=7 confusion matrix has 42 free parameters that cannot be meaningfully collapsed into a single scalar. For these higher-K settings, we provide three interpretability tools: (a) eigendecomposition summaries that identify which modes of category confusion drive downstream bias (e.g., 'the dominant bias mode is confusion between categories 2 and 5'), (b) worst-case scalar bounds that reduce the matrix expression to a conservative scalar threshold, and (c) diagnostic visualizations mapping confusion matrix regions to bias magnitude. This approach extends Veierød & Laake's (2001) insight that multi-category bias depends on all coefficients weighted by the full misclassification matrix, from their regression-only setting to the full DAG-based causal framework. We provide a complete taxonomy: for each topology, which confusion matrix structural properties (symmetry, diagonal dominance, spectral gap, off-diagonal concentration) preserve vs. distort downstream estimates, with explicit algebraic conditions at K=3 and matrix conditions for general K. (2) ANNOTATION SENSITIVITY VALUES (ASVs): Develop a sensitivity analysis framework, extending VanderWeele & Li's (2019) measurement-error sensitivity bounds from binary risk ratios to multi-class confusion matrices. For a given DAG topology, observed confusion matrix from a validation sample, and downstream effect estimate, an ASV computes the minimum degree of differential annotation error needed to change the sign or significance of the estimate. ASVs are parameterized by a reference confusion matrix (estimated from the validation subsample) and a differential-error amplification factor δ: 'annotation error would need to be δ times more differential than observed in the validation sample to overturn this conclusion.' SCOPED PROOF STRATEGY: We provide full formal proofs of ASV monotonicity and sharpness for three priority topologies (confounding, mediation, collider) — these are the most common in CSS research and have the most tractable algebra. For the remaining three topologies (M-bias, instrument, front-door), we provide the ASV expressions and validate monotonicity and coverage properties via Monte Carlo simulation, stating sharpness as conjectures supported by numerical evidence. This honest scoping reflects the substantial algebraic complexity: each topology requires separate treatment, and the multi-class case introduces matrix inequalities that do not trivially generalize from VanderWeele & Li's scalar bounds. Full proofs for the remaining topologies are identified as immediate future work. ASVs are reported alongside downstream estimates, analogous to E-values for unmeasured confounding — giving reviewers and readers an interpretable robustness metric. (3) CORRECTION GUIDANCE FRAMEWORK: Rather than proposing a new correction method, we provide a principled decision framework for selecting among existing methods (DSL, PPI, MLA, naive plug-in) based on the diagnosed DAG topology, error structure, and ASV. When ASV > threshold (conclusion is robust), naive plug-in may suffice. When error is non-differential and binary, MLA is recommended. When error is differential but a validation sample exists, DSL provides design-based guarantees. We characterize the regimes where each method applies and where no existing method is adequate — identifying open problems for future work, including dependence-aware multi-LLM correction that properly handles correlated errors across annotators (Balasubramanian et al. 2026; CARE, Zhao et al. 2026). VALIDATION: Monte Carlo simulations across six DAG topologies with calibrated error parameters from empirical LLM confusion matrices (varying symmetry, differential error degree, and number of categories K=3,5,7), validating ASV coverage properties and bias expression accuracy. Empirical experiments on three CSS tasks — protest event coding (multi-class, K=5), political stance detection (ordinal, K=3), and toxicity annotation (binary, K=2 as special case) — using Llama 4 Maverick, Qwen 3.5, and DeepSeek V4 via API inference against expert gold-standard labels. We demonstrate that (a) multi-class bias expressions correctly predict the direction and approximate magnitude of bias in empirical settings, (b) ASVs provide calibrated robustness indicators with verified coverage for the three fully-proved topologies, and (c) the correction guidance framework selects the appropriate method for each DAG scenario. Release an open-source Python toolkit ('annot-sensitivity') that takes a user-specified DAG, one or more LLM confusion matrices, and a downstream estimate as input and outputs bias expressions (scalar for K≤3, matrix-form with interpretability summaries for K>3), ASV bounds, sensitivity curves, correction recommendations, and diagnostic visualizations.\n43\t\n44\t## Resource Estimate\n45\t\n46\t- **auto_research**: {'api_annotation_cost': '$300-$550', 'claude_api_cost': '$200-$400', 'estimated_total_cost': '$545-$1030', 'gpu_hours': '15-30', 'speed_bottleneck': 'Expert review of ASV sharpness proofs for three priority topologies and bias expression derivations; acquiring gold-standard multi-class human-annotated datasets for empirical validation', 'timeline': '4-6 weeks'}\n47\t- **cost_notes**: API inference costs based on verified May 2026 pricing: Llama 4 Maverick $0.27/$0.85 per Mtok (Together.ai), DeepSeek V4-Flash $0.14/$0.28 per Mtok (DeepSeek API), Qwen 3.5 Plus $0.30/$1.80 per Mtok (OpenRouter). Prices verified against provider pricing pages as of May 2026; note pricing varies across providers (e.g., Fireworks charges $0.22/$0.88 for Maverick). Self-hosting on H100s is unnecessary for annotation-scale workloads. Estimates assume ~30K annotations per model across 3 datasets with 3 prompt variants. Timeline extended by 2 weeks vs. Round 2 to accommodate formal ASV sharpness proofs for confounding, mediation, and collider topologies.\n48\t- **human_in_loop**: {'api_annotation_cost': '$300-$550', 'claude_api_cost': '$100-$200', 'estimated_total_cost': '$445-$830', 'gpu_hours': '15-30', 'timeline': '8-10 weeks'}\n49\t- **manual**: {'api_annotation_cost': '$300-$550 (3 LLMs × 3 datasets × multiple prompt variants via Together.ai/Fireworks/DeepSeek API)', 'estimated_total_cost': '$360-$670', 'gpu_hours': '20-40 (simulations only; annotation via API)', 'timeline': '10-12 weeks'}\n50\t\n51\t## Key References\n52\t\n53\t- Challenges of Using Text Classifiers for Causal Inference (Wood-Doughty, Shpitser & Dredze, EMNLP 2018)\n54\t- Misclassification in Automated Content Analysis Causes Bias in Regression. Can We Fix It? Yes We Can! (TeBlunthuis, Hase & Chan, Communication Methods & Measures 2024)\n55\t- Causal Diagrams and Measurement Bias (Hernán & Cole, American Journal of Epidemiology 2009)\n56\t- Results on Differential and Dependent Measurement Error of the Exposure Using Signed DAGs (VanderWeele & Hernán, American Journal of Epidemiology 2012)\n57\t- Simple Sensitivity Analysis for Differential Measurement Error (VanderWeele & Li, American Journal of Epidemiology 2019)\n58\t- Sensitivity Analysis without Assumptions / E-values (VanderWeele & Ding, Epidemiology 2016; Annals of Internal Medicine 2017)\n59\t- Measurement Bias: A Structural Perspective (Li, Fan, Zhang, Liu, Bao & Zheng, arXiv 2020)\n60\t- Bias due to non-differential misclassification of polytomous confounders (Brenner, Journal of Clinical Epidemiology 1993)\n61\t- Exposure misclassification: bias in category specific Poisson regression coefficients (Veierød & Laake, Statistics in Medicine 2001)\n62\t- Large Language Model Hacking: Quantifying the Hidden Risks of Using LLMs for Text Annotation (Baumann, Röttger, Urman, Wendsjö, Plaza-del-Arco, Gruber & Hovy, arXiv 2025, 2509.08825)\n63\t- Hidden Measurement Error in LLM Pipelines (Messing, arXiv 2026, 2604.11581)\n64\t- Can Unconfident LLM Annotations Be Used for Confident Conclusions? (Gligorić et al., NAACL 2025)\n65\t- Benchmarking Debiasing Methods for LLM-based Parameter Estimates (Jerzak et al., EMNLP 2025)\n66\t- Using Imperfect Surrogates for Downstream Inference: Design-Based Supervised Learning (Egami et al., NeurIPS 2023)\n67\t- Prediction-Powered Inference (Angelopoulos et al., Science 2023)\n68\t- Correlated Errors in Large Language Models (Kim, Garg, Peng & Garg, ICML 2025, arXiv:2506.07962)\n69\t- Dependence-Aware Label Aggregation for LLM-as-a-Judge via Ising Models (Balasubramanian, Podkopaev & Kasiviswanathan, arXiv 2026, 2601.22336)\n70\t- CARE: Confounder-Aware Aggregation for Reliable LLM Evaluation (Zhao, Shin, Huang, Namburi & Sala, arXiv 2026, 2603.00039)\n71\t- Identifiability of Parameters in Latent Structure Models with Many Observed Variables (Allman, Matias & Rhodes, Annals of Statistics 2009)\n72\t- What is a protest anyway? Codebook conceptualization is still a first-order concern in LLM-era classification (Halterman & Keith, arXiv 2025)\n73\t- From Ground Truth to Measurement: A Statistical Framework for Human Labeling (Chew, Eckman, Kern & Kreuter, arXiv 2026, 2604.07591)\n74\t- To Err Is Human; To Annotate, SILICON? Reducing Measurement Error in LLM Annotation (Cheng, Mayya & Sedoc, arXiv 2024, 2412.14461)\n75\t- Multi-Perspective LLM Annotations for Valid Analyses in Subjective Tasks (Mehrotra, Visokay & Gligorić, arXiv 2026, 2603.21404)\n76\t- Graphical Causal Models for Survey Inference (Schuessler & Selb, SMR 2025)\n77\t- Using Large Language Models for Qualitative Analysis can Introduce Serious Bias (Ashwin, Chhabra & Rao, SMR 2025)\n78\t\n79\t## Reviewer Evaluation\n80\t\n81\t**Scores**:\n82\t  - entry_triage_x: **33.0**/5\n83\t  - exit_triage_z: **40.0**/5\n84\t\n85\t## Human Review Note\n86\t\n87\t## External Review — Verdict: PASS\n88\t\n89\t- X (entry triage): 33.0%\n90\t- Y (terminal self-eval): 43.0%\n91\t- Z (exit triage): 40.0%\n92\t- Z interval: [34.0%, 46.0%]\n93\t- Real improvement (Z-X): +7.0 pp\n94\t\n95\t### Optimization Suggestions\n96\t【优化方案】\n97\t\n98\t**一、标题与核心定位（命中 Novelty + Significance）**\n99\t\n100\t1. **[标题]** — 建议修改为 \"Multi-Class Annotation Errors Are Not Created Equal: Bias Taxonomy, Sensitivity Analysis, and Correction Guidance for LLM-Annotated Causal Studies\"，突出 \"not created equal\" 的 taxonomy 核心思想，比原始技术性标题更有 punch — **命中 Novelty 第一印象**\n101\t\n102\t2. **[Introduction 第 1 段 / Motivation]** — 新增 \"ensemble failure → error structure is irreplaceable\" 论证链：Kim et al. (ICML 2025, arXiv:2506.07962) 证明 LLM 跨模型错误高度相关（60% agreement when both err），ensemble 无法逃避系统性偏差 → 研究者必须面对并分析所用模型的具体 confusion matrix 结构 → 这正是 taxonomy 的功能。将 Kim et al. 从 limitation 翻转为 proposal 不可替代性的正面论据 — **命中 Significance（从 \"error structure matters\" 升级为 \"error structure is the ONLY thing you can work with\"）**\n103\t\n104\t3. **[Introduction 第 3-4 段 / Core problem framing]** — 重构核心论点为\"对现有 correction literature 的结构性批判\"：Jerzak et al. (EMNLP 2025) 将 error 视为 monolithic 进行 benchmarking → 如果不同 confusion matrix structure 在不同 DAG topology 下产生质的不同 bias → unconditional benchmarking 本身就是 misleading → 本 proposal 的 taxonomy 是缺失的 conditioning variable。这比原始的\"帮研究者选工具\"定位更有力，因为它指向了现有文献的结构性缺陷 — **命中 Significance + Novelty**\n105\t\n106\t**二、Key Insight 章节（命中 Novelty）**\n107\t\n108\t4. **[Key Insight / Lead Finding]** — 将 \"binary safety net breaks in multi-class\" 提升为 paper 的核心 surprising result，在 Introduction 中以 lead finding 呈现。精确化 Brenner (1993) 引用：原文说 \"in most cases the bias is in the same direction as the confounding\"，guarantee 并非完全消失而是不再可靠。将 nuance 转化为 proposal 的价值点——\"Brenner identifies the danger but cannot tell you when it strikes; our taxonomy resolves this ambiguity with exact conditions for each confusion matrix structure × DAG topology cell\" — **命中 Novelty（防止 overselling 攻击的同时锐化差异化）**\n109\t\n110\t5. **[Key Insight / 质变论证]** — 新增 binarization collapse 不可行论证：审稿人最自然的 trivial alternative 是\"把 K-class 折叠为 binary 再用成熟工具\"。Brenner (1993) 直接回答：binarizing polytomous variable 本身引入方向不可预测的新 bias。补充技术支撑：binary confusion matrix 2 个自由度 → scalar bias；K=5 confusion matrix 20 个自由度 → matrix-valued bias，这是结构性的复杂度跃升而非仅是参数增加 — **命中 Novelty（消除 trivial alternative 质疑）**\n111\t\n112\t**三、Related Work 章节（命中 Related Work）**\n113\t\n114\t6. **[Related Work / 致命遗漏修复]** — 新增 Lee & Wood-Doughty (arXiv:2411.03004, 2024) 引用并明确 differentiate。原始 proposal 未引用此高度相关工作。该论文将 Wood-Doughty (EMNLP 2018) 扩展至 categorical + LLM classifiers（MIMIC 临床数据），但仅覆盖 1 个 DAG topology（unobserved confounding），无 bias taxonomy，无 sensitivity analysis。将其定位为 stepping stone，修正原 proposal 中 \"Wood-Doughty and TeBlunthuis are limited to binary\" 的叙事——binary limitation 已被部分突破，但系统性多 topology 处理仍缺失 — **命中 Related Work（消除审稿人发现未引用高相关论文后直接 reject 的风险）**\n115\t\n116\t7. **[Related Work / 近期文献补全]** — 新增以下 2024-2026 文献（若原始 proposal 未引用）：Cheng, Mayya & Sedoc (arXiv:2412.14461) SILICON error decomposition; Dependence-Aware Label Aggregation via Ising Models (arXiv:2601.22336, AAAI 2027); CARE (arXiv:2603.00039, AAAI 2027); Multi-Perspective LLM Annotations (arXiv:2603.21404, NAACL 2027); Chew et al. (arXiv:2604.07591) annotation-as-measurement; PPI++ (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) / multiPPI++ (COLM 2024); CrossPPI\n117\t\n118\t_Reviewed by external pipeline (batch7), 2026-05-20_\n119\t\n120\t\n121\t## MVP Validation Results\n122\t\n123\t- **Date**: 2026-05-20\n124\t- **Hypothesis Supported**: Yes\n125\t\n126\t### Key Metrics\n127\t\n128\t理论vs MC一致: 5/5 (全部在95%CI内) | 非差异性C偏差范围: 0.204-0.298 (OVB=0.300) | 二分类折叠差异: Δ=0.001-0.089 | 100k随机C方向反转: 0/100000\n129\t\n130\t### Summary\n131\t\n132\tConfounding DAG + K=3 三分类标注偏差验证。推导了 OLS 中 β_T 的理论偏差闭式表达式，在 5 种混淆矩阵结构（对称高精度/非对称/结构性混淆/近随机/差异性）× 1000 MC 重复 × N=10,000 下全部验证通过。附加 100k 随机 C 矩阵搜索发现 confounding DAG 下偏差方向始终与 OVB 同向（单调），方向反转需要在 mediator/collider 等其他拓扑验证。二分类折叠对照证实多分类控制保留更多混杂信息（最大 Δ=0.089）。\n133\t\n134\t### Recommendation\n135\t\n136\tGO — 理论框架验证通过，多分类≠二分类已量化证实。方向反转（核心 claim 最强形式）需 research 阶段在 mediator/collider DAG 上验证，confounding DAG 的单调性本身是有价值的 characterization result。建议立即进入 research 阶段，优先扩展到 6 种 DAG 拓扑。\n137\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"898815c0-f847-4638-9e6c-fec5f265ed2f","uuid":"cb75689a-c3dc-477d-9bdc-ee1731c48cbc","timestamp":"2026-05-20T07:30:53.965Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod_0510/projects/causal_annot_error_attrib/docs/IDEA_BRIEF.md","content":"# Idea Brief: When Do LLM Annotation Errors Matter? Multi-Class Bias Expressions and Annotation Sensitivity Values for Downstream Social Science Inference\n\n\n- **Idea ID**: `idea_027`\n- **Created**: 2026-05-20\n- **Approved**: 2026-05-20\n- **Source**: foreign:be-idea-gen-0515-4\n- **Level**: solid\n- **Target Venues**: ARR October 2026 (deadline: ~2026-10-15; commit to NAACL 2027 or later *ACL venues), AAAI 2027 (abstract: 2026-07-21, paper: 2026-07-28), ICWSM 2027 Round 2 (deadline: 2026-09-15)\n\n## Seed Topic\n\nweb_research\n\n## Generation Journey\n\n1. **Start** — 开始生成，seed: '## Seed 4: Open-Vocabulary Affordance Zero-Shot VLA\\n\\nObjective: CoRL 2026 submission.\\n\\nDirection: Traditional affordance methods and VLAs rely on fixed category annotations and struggle with open-world unknown objects and free-form interaction instructions. CoA-VLA (arXiv 2412.20451, December 2024) integrates affordance chain-of-reasoning into VLA, but uses 4 predefined structured affordance types (object, grasp, spatial, motion) — not truly open-vocabulary — and cannot handle free-form interaction descriptions like \"use this thing to wipe off the dust over there.\" GeneralVLA (arXiv 2602.04315, February 2026) achieves zero-shot affordance perception and manipulation trajectory generation through VLM+SAM, but is a modular three-stage pipeline (affordance perception → trajectory planning → low-level control), not a unified end-to-end VLA — with information loss between modules and inability to jointly optimize. OVAL-Prompt (ICRA 2024 VLMNM Workshop, arXiv 2404.11000) achieves truly open-vocabulary affordance localization, but only performs detection/localization without generating actions. AffordDP (CVPR 2025, arXiv 2412.03142) achieves affordance cross-category transfer through 3D contact points and trajectories, but completely lacks language conditioning. Core gap: No work unifies open-vocabulary affordance reasoning into an end-to-end VLA for zero-shot manipulation — existing work either has fixed affordance types (CoA-VLA), or is a modular pipeline (GeneralVLA), or only does detection without actions (OVAL-Prompt), or lacks language (AffordDP).\\n\\nSeed Papers:\\n- \"OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\" (Kim et al., CoRL 2024, arXiv 2406.09246)\\n  — 7B parameter open-source VLA, trained on 970K real robot demonstrations, establishing the VLA open-source paradigm. The base architecture for this work — integrating open-vocabulary affordance reasoning on top.\\n- \"CoA-VLA: Improving Vision-Language-Action Models via Visual-Textual Chain-of-Affordance\" (Li et al., arXiv 2412.20451, December 2024)\\n  — Integrates affordance chain-of-reasoning into VLA: model sequentially reasons about object affordance, grasp affordance, spatial affordance, and motion affordance before predicting actions. Uses visual-language collaborative injection module. Surpasses OpenVLA and Octo. Key limitation: 4 affordance types are predefined/structured, not open-vocabulary — cannot handle arbitrary natural language descriptions of novel interactions.\\n- \"GeneralVLA: Generalizable Vision-Language-Action Models with Knowledge-Guided Trajectory Planning\" (Ma et al., arXiv 2602.04315, February 2026)\\n  — Hierarchical three-module pipeline: (1) VLM+SAM affordance perception for keypoint detection, (2) mid-level planning agent for trajectory generation, (3) low-level control policy. Achieves zero-shot manipulation across 14 tasks without real-world data. Key limitation: Modular pipeline, not end-to-end — affordance module, trajectory planner, and controller are separated, with information bottlenecks between modules.\\n- \"OVAL-Prompt: Open-Vocabulary Affordance Localization for Robot Manipulation through LLM Affordance-Grounding\" (Tong et al., ICRA 2024 VLMNM Workshop, arXiv 2404.11000)\\n  — Prompt-based method combining VLM for object part segmentation and LLM for part-affordance grounding mapping, achieving open-vocabulary affordance localization without prior training. Competitive with supervised methods on localization benchmarks. Key limitation: Only performs affordance detection/localization, does not generate robot actions — a perception module, not a complete manipulation system.\\n- \"AffordDP: Generalizable Diffusion Policy with Transferable Affordance\" (Wu et al., CVPR 2025, arXiv 2412.03142)\\n  — Models manipulation priors through 3D contact points and post-contact trajectories, achieving affordance cross-category transfer using visual models + point cloud registration. Affordance-guided diffusion policy generates actions. Key limitation: No language conditioning — affordances are geometric/contact-based, not language-driven, cannot handle open-vocabulary instructions.\\n\\nKnown Gap:\\n- CoA-VLA integrates affordance into VLA but uses predefined structured types, does not support open vocabulary\\n- GeneralVLA does zero-shot affordance manipulation but is a modular pipeline, not end-to-end\\n- OVAL-Prompt does open-vocabulary affordance but only detection, no action generation\\n- AffordDP does affordance cross-category transfer but no language conditioning\\n- VoxPoser (CoRL 2023, arXiv 2307.05973) uses LLM to generate 3D affordance/constraint maps but is modular, no end-to-end learning\\n- No work unifies open-vocabulary affordance reasoning into end-to-end VLA\\n\\nIn Scope: Methods unifying open-vocabulary affordance reasoning into end-to-end VLA architecture. Specifically includes: text prompt-driven open-vocabulary affordance feature activation mechanism, universal shape-function-interaction attribute association learning, end-to-end joint training of affordance reasoning and action generation, zero-shot generalization to unseen object categories and novel language interaction instructions. The core contribution should be a unified architecture, not a modular concatenation of affordance detection + action generation.\\n\\nOut of Scope: Predefined fixed affordance types (CoA-VLA already done). Modular affordance pipelines (GeneralVLA/VoxPoser already done). Affordance detection only without actions (OVAL-Prompt already done). Affordance transfer without language conditioning (AffordDP already done). Pure affordance benchmarking/analysis.\\n\\nContribution Type: Method contribution. Output must include (1) unified architecture design for open-vocabulary affordance and VLA, (2) end-to-end training strategy for affordance reasoning and action generation, (3) experimental validation of zero-shot generalization on unseen objects and novel interaction instructions, demonstrating advantages over structured affordance VLA and modular methods.\\n\\nEvaluation Protocol:\\nTraining: Based on OpenVLA architecture, integrating open-vocabulary affordance module. Training data uses LIBERO + affordance annotation data (e.g., AGD20K/UMD Affordance).\\nEvaluation Metrics:\\n- Primary metric: Zero-shot manipulation success rate on unseen object categories\\n- Novel interaction instruction generalization: Language interaction descriptions unseen during training (e.g., train on \"pick up,\" test on \"scoop out\")\\n- Affordance localization precision: Compare with detection methods like OVAL-Prompt (ensure affordance understanding itself does not degrade)\\n- Manipulation success rate: Compare with standard VLA baselines (ensure adding affordance does not degrade known task performance)\\n- Baseline comparisons:\\n  1. Affordance-free VLA (OpenVLA direct fine-tuning)\\n  2. Structured affordance VLA (CoA-VLA reproduction)\\n  3. Modular affordance pipeline (GeneralVLA approach)\\n  4. Affordance detection + independent policy (OVAL-Prompt + policy learning)\\n  5. LLM affordance map + planning (VoxPoser approach)\\n- Ablation studies: Open-vocabulary affordance vs. fixed-type affordance, end-to-end vs. staged training, text-driven affordance vs. vision-driven affordance\\n- Cross-object transfer tests: Shape-function association generalization (e.g., if trained on shovel\\'s \"scooping\" affordance, can the model activate the same interaction for novel shovel-like tools?)\\n- Free-form instruction tests: Non-templated natural language instruction understanding\\n\\nBuilding Blocks: OpenVLA codebase (VLA base architecture); CoA-VLA codebase (affordance VLA baseline); OVAL-Prompt codebase (open-vocabulary affordance detection reference); AGD20K/UMD Affordance datasets (affordance annotations); LIBERO benchmark (manipulation task evaluation); DINOv2/SigLIP visual encoders; SAM2 (segmentation); HuggingFace transformers + accelerate; CLIP/SigLIP (open-vocabulary semantic alignment).\\n\\nHard Constraints:\\nCompute: ≤200 H100-hours. Strategy: Fine-tune based on 7B VLA, affordance module leveraging pre-trained VLM features (e.g., CLIP/SigLIP) to reduce training requirements. Training ~80 H100-hours, baseline reproduction ~50 H100-hours, ablation ~40 H100-hours, generalization testing ~30 H100-hours.\\nEvaluation: Must validate zero-shot generalization on unseen object categories. Must include free-form language instruction tests. Must include ≥4 baselines. Must report both affordance precision and manipulation success rate. Must include ablation studies and cross-object transfer tests.\\n\\nExploration Directions: (1) End-to-end activation of VLM internal affordance features: Rather than adding an external affordance detection module to VLA, activate interaction-relevant visual features within VLA\\'s VLM backbone network directly through language instructions. Key insight: Large-scale VLMs (e.g., PaliGemma, SigLIP) have already learned extensive shape-function associations during pre-training (\"knives can cut,\" \"bowls can hold\"), only requiring suitable activation mechanisms to release this implicit knowledge. (2) Affordance as intermediate reasoning tokens: Rather than explicitly outputting affordance heatmaps, have VLA generate a set of \"affordance reasoning tokens\" before generating action token sequences (similar to CoA-VLA\\'s chain reasoning but open-vocabulary). These tokens encode interaction possibilities of objects in the current scene, then condition subsequent action generation. Differs from CoA-VLA: not limited to 4 predefined types; reasoning content is dynamically determined by language instructions. (3) Contrastive learning for universal shape-function-interaction mapping: Add contrastive objective during VLA training to pull together different-shaped objects with similar functions (various \"tools that can scoop things\") in affordance feature space, and push apart similar-shaped objects with different functions (decorative bowl vs. functional bowl). More flexible than CoA-VLA\\'s structured types — any natural language-described interaction can participate in contrasting. (4) Knowledge distillation for zero-shot affordance reasoning: Use large VLMs\\' (e.g., GPT-4V level) zero-shot affordance reasoning capability (\"how can this object be interacted with?\") as teacher signals, distilling into small VLA. Large models have rich commonsense knowledge and can generate affordance descriptions for virtually any object, but are too large to deploy directly on robots. (5) Affordance consistency self-supervision: The same object under different language descriptions should have consistent affordance responses (\"grab the handle\" vs. \"pick it up by the stick part\" describe the same interaction). Use this consistency as self-supervision signals, reducing dependency on fine-grained affordance annotations.\\n\\nAvoid:\\n- Using predefined fixed affordance types — duplicates CoA-VLA\\n- Modular pipeline (affordance detection → planning → control) — duplicates GeneralVLA/VoxPoser, and prevents end-to-end optimization\\n- Only doing affordance detection without actions — duplicates OVAL-Prompt\\n- Ignoring CoA-VLA baseline — it is the most recent affordance + VLA work; must directly compare\\n- Ignoring GeneralVLA — it is the most recent zero-shot affordance manipulation work; must clarify advantages of end-to-end vs. modular\\n- Reporting results only on within-training-distribution objects — the core value of open vocabulary is on out-of-distribution objects\\n- Over-relying on affordance annotation data — should design methods to reduce annotation requirements\\n\\nIdea Generation Quality Constraints (Hard rules based on historical cases):\\n\\n1. No strawman problems: Any claim of \"existing methods assume X\" or \"existing methods fail on Y\" must cite specific paragraphs from specific papers as evidence.\\n\\n2. No pure observation ideas: Output must include a complete method, not merely analysis/benchmark/empirical study.\\n\\n3. No mediocre methods: Methods must answer:\\n   - Core insight: Large-scale VLMs have already implicitly learned rich shape-function association knowledge during pre-training; CoA-VLA released part of it through structured reasoning chains, but is limited by predefined types. We directly activate corresponding affordance features within VLA through open-vocabulary language instructions, fully releasing VLM\\'s implicit shape-function knowledge into action generation.\\n   - Why non-obvious: (1) CoA-VLA (December 2024) is the first work integrating affordance chain reasoning into VLA; affordance + VLA itself is a new paradigm; (2) open-vocabulary requires solving the infinite-dimensional affordance space problem — unlike 4 fixed types that can be directly classified; (3) end-to-end training of open-vocabulary affordance requires affordance annotation data, but such data is scarce — needs training strategies that reduce annotation dependency.\\n   - What information is exploited: Implicit shape-function association knowledge from VLM pre-training — which neither 2D visual VLAs nor fixed-type affordance methods fully utilize.\\n   - Mechanistic argument: When given a free-form language instruction (e.g., \"scoop the rice\"), the VLM internally already encodes knowledge that \"scooping requires a concave container-shaped tool.\" Through suitable activation mechanisms, this knowledge can be transformed into affordance attention in visual feature space — focusing on regions in the scene with corresponding geometric functional properties, then conditioning action generation. More flexible than CoA-VLA\\'s fixed 4-type reasoning because affordance is dynamically defined by language.\\n\\n4. No duplicating existing work: Each core technical component must be confirmed with at least 3 different search queries to have no directly related work.\\n\\n5. No padding experiments:\\n   - \"Delete experiment test\": Keep only the method description — does it teach the reader something non-trivial? Yes — VLM implicit shape-function knowledge can drive VLA action generation through open-vocabulary activation.\\n   - \"Baseline fear test\": If CoA-VLA\\'s 4 structured types already cover most scenarios, is the paper still valuable? Must prove open-vocabulary methods\\' advantages on free-form instructions and novel interactions.\\n   - \"Citation test\": Will people cite the numbers or the insight 5 years later? The insight — open-vocabulary affordance in end-to-end VLA paradigm.\\n\\n6. The following method patterns are rejected (unless with extremely strong structural reasons):\\n   - \"Add module X\" — Why this specific structure?\\n   - \"Train on data Y\" — Is data construction itself a contribution?\\n   - \"Transfer Z from domain A to domain B\" — What is the structural analogy?\\n   - \"Combine P and Q\" — Where is the synergy? Combining open-vocabulary detection (OVAL-Prompt) with VLA — must prove end-to-end joint training outperforms simple concatenation.\\n   - \"Use more/larger/stronger X\" — Scale is not a method contribution.\\n\\nCompetition Warning: CoA-VLA (December 2024) is the most recent affordance + VLA — uses structured reasoning chains but not open-vocabulary. GeneralVLA (February 2026) does zero-shot affordance manipulation but is a modular pipeline. OVAL-Prompt (ICRA 2024 Workshop) does open-vocabulary affordance detection but no actions. AffordDP (CVPR 2025) does affordance cross-category transfer but no language. VoxPoser (CoRL 2023) uses LLM to generate 3D affordance maps but is modular. π0 (Physical Intelligence) and RT-2 and other large VLAs may implicitly contain partial affordance knowledge but do not explicitly leverage it. Differentiation core: The only work integrating open-vocabulary affordance reasoning end-to-end into a unified VLA architecture. Highest risk: CoA-VLA is closest in space; if its author team quickly extends to open-vocabulary, differentiation shrinks. Monitor: CoA-VLA author team, GeneralVLA author team, Physical Intelligence (whether π0 follow-ups will integrate affordance).\\n\\nTags: direction=open_vocab_affordance_zero_shot_vla, contribution_type=method, sub_direction=unified_affordance_vla_end_to_end, target_venues=[\"CoRL 2026\"].'\n2. **Problem Discovery** (492s) — 发现 4 个研究方向\n   - Open-Vocabulary Affordance Chain-of-Reasoning for End-to-End VLA\n   - Activating Implicit VLM Affordance Knowledge for Manipulation\n   - Affordance-Conditioned Flow Matching for Open-Vocabulary VLA\n   - Functional Correspondence Learning for Zero-Shot Affordance Transfer\n3. **Method Synthesis** (626s) — 完成: \"Open-Vocabulary Affordance Chain-of-Reasoning for End-to-End VLA\" [top-tier] → ICLR 2027 (abstract Sep 19, paper Sep 24, 2026) — top-tier ML venue, best fit for method contribution, AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28, 2026) — top-tier AI venue, earlier deadline, CoRL 2026 (abstract May 26, paper May 29, 2026) — top-tier robotics venue, tight but feasible with auto-research, CVPR 2027 (abstract Nov 7, paper Nov 13, 2026) — top-tier vision venue, fallback if earlier submissions need revision\n3. **Method Synthesis** (813s) — 完成: \"Activating Implicit VLM Affordance Knowledge for Manipulation\" [top-tier] → ICLR 2027 (abstract Sep 19, paper Sep 24, 2026 — highest-tier ML venue, ideal for the VLM interpretability + VLA generalization contribution), CoRL 2026 (abstract May 26, paper May 29, 2026 — premier robotics learning venue, tight deadline but perfect topical fit), AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28, 2026 — broad top-tier AI venue, comfortable timeline)\n3. **Method Synthesis** (4s) — 完成: \"Affordance-Conditioned Flow Matching for Open-Vocabulary VLA\"\n3. **Method Synthesis** (4s) — 完成: \"Functional Correspondence Learning for Zero-Shot Affordance Transfer\"\n\n## Core Problem\n\nLLM annotation errors can bias downstream social science conclusions, but researchers currently lack tools to answer two critical questions: (1) which specific annotation error structures actually propagate bias under my study's causal design, and (2) how severe must differential annotation error be to overturn my conclusion? This is not a hypothetical concern: Baumann, Röttger et al. (2025) demonstrate empirically across 37 tasks from 21 studies that approximately 31% of downstream conclusions are incorrect when using SOTA LLM annotations, rising to 50% for smaller models. Existing causal frameworks for text classifier measurement error (Wood-Doughty et al. 2018; TeBlunthuis et al. 2024) address the first question only for binary classification in limited DAG topologies, while sensitivity analysis tools analogous to E-values (VanderWeele & Ding 2017) or measurement error bounds (VanderWeele & Li 2019) do not exist for annotation-induced bias. The correction methods available (DSL, PPI, MLA) each assume specific error structures, but without diagnostic tools to characterize error structure, researchers cannot determine which correction is appropriate — or whether correction is needed at all. Meanwhile, the epidemiological measurement-error literature (Hernán & Cole 2009; VanderWeele & Hernán 2012) provides the causal foundations but has not been applied to the multi-class LLM annotation setting where confusion matrices replace simple misclassification rates.\n\n## Opportunity / Why Now\n\nTwo converging gaps create a focused opportunity. First, the diagnostic gap: Wood-Doughty et al. (2018) produce no formal bias theorems and defer identifiability conditions, and TeBlunthuis et al. (2024) provide correction only for binary outcomes with a single classifier — yet most social science annotation tasks involve multi-class labels (protest type, stance category, topic), and bias propagation in the multi-class case is qualitatively different because directional bias depends on which categories are confused, not just overall error rates. The epidemiological literature confirms this complexity: Brenner (1993) shows that non-differential misclassification of polytomous confounders can bias in either direction, and Veierød & Laake (2001) prove that multi-category exposure misclassification produces bias that depends on all true category-specific coefficients weighted by the full misclassification probability matrix. No systematic treatment exists for how K-class confusion matrix structures map to downstream bias across different causal designs in the text annotation context. Second, the sensitivity gap: no sensitivity analysis framework exists for annotation-induced bias — researchers cannot report 'how differential must annotation error be to overturn my conclusion?' VanderWeele & Li (2019) provide sensitivity bounds for differential measurement error, but only for binary exposure/outcome settings using risk ratios, and explicitly do not address polytomous extensions or confusion-matrix parameterizations. Bridging these two gaps — extending bias expressions to multi-class annotation and providing annotation-specific sensitivity analysis — would give social science researchers actionable diagnostic tools to evaluate the robustness of LLM-annotated studies, complementing existing correction methods rather than replacing them. The urgency is underscored by Baumann et al. (2025), who show that 31% of downstream conclusions from LLM annotations are already incorrect — researchers need diagnostic tools now.\n\n## Landscape (SOTA & Limitations)\n\nThe causal treatment of measurement error in DAGs is well-established in epidemiology: Hernán & Cole (2009) formalize starred-node DAGs with differential/non-differential and independent/dependent error taxonomy; VanderWeele & Hernán (2012) prove bias-direction results using signed DAGs; Li et al. (2020) connect causal structure between true variables to the type of measurement error that arises; VanderWeele & Li (2019) provide sensitivity bounds for differential measurement error of binary exposure/outcome using risk ratios; Schuessler & Selb (SMR 2025) apply DAGs to survey measurement error. For polytomous variables specifically, Brenner (1993) analyzes non-differential misclassification of polytomous confounders showing bias can go in either direction, and Veierød & Laake (2001) derive bias expressions for multi-category exposure misclassification in Poisson regression, showing bias in each category depends on all true coefficients weighted by misclassification probabilities — but neither connects to DAG-based causal reasoning or sensitivity analysis. In NLP, Wood-Doughty, Shpitser & Dredze (EMNLP 2018) apply DAG reasoning to text classifier errors with matrix adjustment correction but produce no formal bias theorems, no identifiability conditions, and note severe instability in corrections. TeBlunthuis, Hase & Chan (CMM 2024) provide the most complete treatment: four DAG scenarios (differential/non-differential × IV/DV), testable conditions via likelihood ratio tests, and a Maximum Likelihood Adjustment (MLA) method — but only for binary classification with a single classifier, and without LLM-specific considerations. On the LLM annotation side, Baumann, Röttger et al. (2025, arXiv:2509.08825) provide the strongest empirical motivation for diagnostic tools: across 37 annotation tasks from 21 published social science studies and 13 million labels from 18 models, they find approximately 31% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with SOTA LLMs, rising to 50% for smaller models, coining the term 'LLM hacking' to describe how configuration choices can make 'virtually anything statistically significant.' Messing (April 2026, arXiv:2604.11581) decomposes LLM pipeline variance via linear mixed models, finding item-judge interaction dominates at 44%, but explicitly excludes causal identification. Gligorić et al. (NAACL 2025) note correlated errors bias estimates and propose DSL/PPI corrections. Jerzak et al. (EMNLP 2025) benchmark correction methods but treat error as monolithic. Halterman & Keith (arXiv 2025) show conceptualization-induced bias cannot be fixed by accuracy improvements alone. Chew et al. (arXiv 2026, 2604.07591) decompose human labeling variance but provide no causal-propagation framework. Cheng, Mayya & Sedoc (arXiv 2024, 2412.14461) decompose LLM annotation error into guideline-, baseline-, prompt-, and model-induced components via their SILICON framework but do not connect to causal propagation. Crucially, recent work on correlated LLM errors reveals that the assumption of independent errors across LLMs is violated in practice: Kim, Garg, Peng & Garg (ICML 2025, arXiv:2506.07962) show models agree 60% of the time when both err, with larger models having highly correlated errors even across architectures; Balasubramanian, Podkopaev & Kasiviswanathan (arXiv 2026, 2601.22336) formalize dependence-aware aggregation via Ising models, proving conditional-independence methods are strictly suboptimal; CARE (Zhao et al., arXiv 2026, 2603.00039) demonstrates confounder-aware aggregation with identifiability guarantees under shared confounders. Mehrotra, Visokay & Gligorić (arXiv 2026, 2603.21404) introduce perspective-driven inference for subjective annotation tasks. No existing work provides: (a) multi-class extension of bias expressions across canonical social-science DAG topologies, or (b) sensitivity analysis values for annotation-induced bias parameterized by confusion matrices.\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nWe extend the causal measurement-error framework for text annotation in two directions, building explicitly on Wood-Doughty et al. (2018), TeBlunthuis et al. (2024), and the epidemiological sensitivity analysis tradition (VanderWeele & Li 2019), with connections to the polytomous misclassification literature (Brenner 1993; Veierød & Laake 2001). (1) MULTI-CLASS BIAS EXPRESSIONS IN MATRIX FORM: Model the LLM annotation process as a noisy measurement node A* within a structural causal model connecting true multi-class label A to covariates X, treatment T, and outcome Y, parameterizing the error mechanism via the full K×K confusion matrix C where C_{ji} = P(A*=j|A=i,X,T). Derive bias expressions for six canonical social-science DAG topologies (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias, instrument, front-door), proving formally which confusion matrix structures produce downstream-innocuous vs. downstream-toxic error for each topology. IMPORTANT QUALIFICATION ON TRACTABILITY: For K=2 (binary), our expressions reduce to the scalar formulas of TeBlunthuis et al. (2024), providing a consistency check. For K=3, we provide fully worked scalar closed-form expressions that practitioners can directly compute and interpret — this is the 'sweet spot' covering common annotation tasks like stance detection (pro/neutral/anti) and sentiment (positive/neutral/negative). For K≥5, bias expressions are necessarily in matrix notation involving confusion matrix algebra (products, inversions, spectral decompositions) rather than scalar closed-form, because a K=7 confusion matrix has 42 free parameters that cannot be meaningfully collapsed into a single scalar. For these higher-K settings, we provide three interpretability tools: (a) eigendecomposition summaries that identify which modes of category confusion drive downstream bias (e.g., 'the dominant bias mode is confusion between categories 2 and 5'), (b) worst-case scalar bounds that reduce the matrix expression to a conservative scalar threshold, and (c) diagnostic visualizations mapping confusion matrix regions to bias magnitude. This approach extends Veierød & Laake's (2001) insight that multi-category bias depends on all coefficients weighted by the full misclassification matrix, from their regression-only setting to the full DAG-based causal framework. We provide a complete taxonomy: for each topology, which confusion matrix structural properties (symmetry, diagonal dominance, spectral gap, off-diagonal concentration) preserve vs. distort downstream estimates, with explicit algebraic conditions at K=3 and matrix conditions for general K. (2) ANNOTATION SENSITIVITY VALUES (ASVs): Develop a sensitivity analysis framework, extending VanderWeele & Li's (2019) measurement-error sensitivity bounds from binary risk ratios to multi-class confusion matrices. For a given DAG topology, observed confusion matrix from a validation sample, and downstream effect estimate, an ASV computes the minimum degree of differential annotation error needed to change the sign or significance of the estimate. ASVs are parameterized by a reference confusion matrix (estimated from the validation subsample) and a differential-error amplification factor δ: 'annotation error would need to be δ times more differential than observed in the validation sample to overturn this conclusion.' SCOPED PROOF STRATEGY: We provide full formal proofs of ASV monotonicity and sharpness for three priority topologies (confounding, mediation, collider) — these are the most common in CSS research and have the most tractable algebra. For the remaining three topologies (M-bias, instrument, front-door), we provide the ASV expressions and validate monotonicity and coverage properties via Monte Carlo simulation, stating sharpness as conjectures supported by numerical evidence. This honest scoping reflects the substantial algebraic complexity: each topology requires separate treatment, and the multi-class case introduces matrix inequalities that do not trivially generalize from VanderWeele & Li's scalar bounds. Full proofs for the remaining topologies are identified as immediate future work. ASVs are reported alongside downstream estimates, analogous to E-values for unmeasured confounding — giving reviewers and readers an interpretable robustness metric. (3) CORRECTION GUIDANCE FRAMEWORK: Rather than proposing a new correction method, we provide a principled decision framework for selecting among existing methods (DSL, PPI, MLA, naive plug-in) based on the diagnosed DAG topology, error structure, and ASV. When ASV > threshold (conclusion is robust), naive plug-in may suffice. When error is non-differential and binary, MLA is recommended. When error is differential but a validation sample exists, DSL provides design-based guarantees. We characterize the regimes where each method applies and where no existing method is adequate — identifying open problems for future work, including dependence-aware multi-LLM correction that properly handles correlated errors across annotators (Balasubramanian et al. 2026; CARE, Zhao et al. 2026). VALIDATION: Monte Carlo simulations across six DAG topologies with calibrated error parameters from empirical LLM confusion matrices (varying symmetry, differential error degree, and number of categories K=3,5,7), validating ASV coverage properties and bias expression accuracy. Empirical experiments on three CSS tasks — protest event coding (multi-class, K=5), political stance detection (ordinal, K=3), and toxicity annotation (binary, K=2 as special case) — using Llama 4 Maverick, Qwen 3.5, and DeepSeek V4 via API inference against expert gold-standard labels. We demonstrate that (a) multi-class bias expressions correctly predict the direction and approximate magnitude of bias in empirical settings, (b) ASVs provide calibrated robustness indicators with verified coverage for the three fully-proved topologies, and (c) the correction guidance framework selects the appropriate method for each DAG scenario. Release an open-source Python toolkit ('annot-sensitivity') that takes a user-specified DAG, one or more LLM confusion matrices, and a downstream estimate as input and outputs bias expressions (scalar for K≤3, matrix-form with interpretability summaries for K>3), ASV bounds, sensitivity curves, correction recommendations, and diagnostic visualizations.\n\n## Resource Estimate\n\n- **auto_research**: {'api_annotation_cost': '$300-$550', 'claude_api_cost': '$200-$400', 'estimated_total_cost': '$545-$1030', 'gpu_hours': '15-30', 'speed_bottleneck': 'Expert review of ASV sharpness proofs for three priority topologies and bias expression derivations; acquiring gold-standard multi-class human-annotated datasets for empirical validation', 'timeline': '4-6 weeks'}\n- **cost_notes**: API inference costs based on verified May 2026 pricing: Llama 4 Maverick $0.27/$0.85 per Mtok (Together.ai), DeepSeek V4-Flash $0.14/$0.28 per Mtok (DeepSeek API), Qwen 3.5 Plus $0.30/$1.80 per Mtok (OpenRouter). Prices verified against provider pricing pages as of May 2026; note pricing varies across providers (e.g., Fireworks charges $0.22/$0.88 for Maverick). Self-hosting on H100s is unnecessary for annotation-scale workloads. Estimates assume ~30K annotations per model across 3 datasets with 3 prompt variants. Timeline extended by 2 weeks vs. Round 2 to accommodate formal ASV sharpness proofs for confounding, mediation, and collider topologies.\n- **human_in_loop**: {'api_annotation_cost': '$300-$550', 'claude_api_cost': '$100-$200', 'estimated_total_cost': '$445-$830', 'gpu_hours': '15-30', 'timeline': '8-10 weeks'}\n- **manual**: {'api_annotation_cost': '$300-$550 (3 LLMs × 3 datasets × multiple prompt variants via Together.ai/Fireworks/DeepSeek API)', 'estimated_total_cost': '$360-$670', 'gpu_hours': '20-40 (simulations only; annotation via API)', 'timeline': '10-12 weeks'}\n\n## Key References\n\n- Challenges of Using Text Classifiers for Causal Inference (Wood-Doughty, Shpitser & Dredze, EMNLP 2018)\n- Misclassification in Automated Content Analysis Causes Bias in Regression. Can We Fix It? Yes We Can! (TeBlunthuis, Hase & Chan, Communication Methods & Measures 2024)\n- Causal Diagrams and Measurement Bias (Hernán & Cole, American Journal of Epidemiology 2009)\n- Results on Differential and Dependent Measurement Error of the Exposure Using Signed DAGs (VanderWeele & Hernán, American Journal of Epidemiology 2012)\n- Simple Sensitivity Analysis for Differential Measurement Error (VanderWeele & Li, American Journal of Epidemiology 2019)\n- Sensitivity Analysis without Assumptions / E-values (VanderWeele & Ding, Epidemiology 2016; Annals of Internal Medicine 2017)\n- Measurement Bias: A Structural Perspective (Li, Fan, Zhang, Liu, Bao & Zheng, arXiv 2020)\n- Bias due to non-differential misclassification of polytomous confounders (Brenner, Journal of Clinical Epidemiology 1993)\n- Exposure misclassification: bias in category specific Poisson regression coefficients (Veierød & Laake, Statistics in Medicine 2001)\n- Large Language Model Hacking: Quantifying the Hidden Risks of Using LLMs for Text Annotation (Baumann, Röttger, Urman, Wendsjö, Plaza-del-Arco, Gruber & Hovy, arXiv 2025, 2509.08825)\n- Hidden Measurement Error in LLM Pipelines (Messing, arXiv 2026, 2604.11581)\n- Can Unconfident LLM Annotations Be Used for Confident Conclusions? (Gligorić et al., NAACL 2025)\n- Benchmarking Debiasing Methods for LLM-based Parameter Estimates (Jerzak et al., EMNLP 2025)\n- Using Imperfect Surrogates for Downstream Inference: Design-Based Supervised Learning (Egami et al., NeurIPS 2023)\n- Prediction-Powered Inference (Angelopoulos et al., Science 2023)\n- Correlated Errors in Large Language Models (Kim, Garg, Peng & Garg, ICML 2025, arXiv:2506.07962)\n- Dependence-Aware Label Aggregation for LLM-as-a-Judge via Ising Models (Balasubramanian, Podkopaev & Kasiviswanathan, arXiv 2026, 2601.22336)\n- CARE: Confounder-Aware Aggregation for Reliable LLM Evaluation (Zhao, Shin, Huang, Namburi & Sala, arXiv 2026, 2603.00039)\n- Identifiability of Parameters in Latent Structure Models with Many Observed Variables (Allman, Matias & Rhodes, Annals of Statistics 2009)\n- What is a protest anyway? Codebook conceptualization is still a first-order concern in LLM-era classification (Halterman & Keith, arXiv 2025)\n- From Ground Truth to Measurement: A Statistical Framework for Human Labeling (Chew, Eckman, Kern & Kreuter, arXiv 2026, 2604.07591)\n- To Err Is Human; To Annotate, SILICON? Reducing Measurement Error in LLM Annotation (Cheng, Mayya & Sedoc, arXiv 2024, 2412.14461)\n- Multi-Perspective LLM Annotations for Valid Analyses in Subjective Tasks (Mehrotra, Visokay & Gligorić, arXiv 2026, 2603.21404)\n- Graphical Causal Models for Survey Inference (Schuessler & Selb, SMR 2025)\n- Using Large Language Models for Qualitative Analysis can Introduce Serious Bias (Ashwin, Chhabra & Rao, SMR 2025)\n\n## Reviewer Evaluation\n\n**Scores**:\n  - entry_triage_x: **33.0**/5\n  - exit_triage_z: **40.0**/5\n\n## Human Review Note\n\n## External Review — Verdict: PASS\n\n- X (entry triage): 33.0%\n- Y (terminal self-eval): 43.0%\n- Z (exit triage): 40.0%\n- Z interval: [34.0%, 46.0%]\n- Real improvement (Z-X): +7.0 pp\n\n### Optimization Suggestions\n【优化方案】\n\n**一、标题与核心定位（命中 Novelty + Significance）**\n\n1. **[标题]** — 建议修改为 \"Multi-Class Annotation Errors Are Not Created Equal: Bias Taxonomy, Sensitivity Analysis, and Correction Guidance for LLM-Annotated Causal Studies\"，突出 \"not created equal\" 的 taxonomy 核心思想，比原始技术性标题更有 punch — **命中 Novelty 第一印象**\n\n2. **[Introduction 第 1 段 / Motivation]** — 新增 \"ensemble failure → error structure is irreplaceable\" 论证链：Kim et al. (ICML 2025, arXiv:2506.07962) 证明 LLM 跨模型错误高度相关（60% agreement when both err），ensemble 无法逃避系统性偏差 → 研究者必须面对并分析所用模型的具体 confusion matrix 结构 → 这正是 taxonomy 的功能。将 Kim et al. 从 limitation 翻转为 proposal 不可替代性的正面论据 — **命中 Significance（从 \"error structure matters\" 升级为 \"error structure is the ONLY thing you can work with\"）**\n\n3. **[Introduction 第 3-4 段 / Core problem framing]** — 重构核心论点为\"对现有 correction literature 的结构性批判\"：Jerzak et al. (EMNLP 2025) 将 error 视为 monolithic 进行 benchmarking → 如果不同 confusion matrix structure 在不同 DAG topology 下产生质的不同 bias → unconditional benchmarking 本身就是 misleading → 本 proposal 的 taxonomy 是缺失的 conditioning variable。这比原始的\"帮研究者选工具\"定位更有力，因为它指向了现有文献的结构性缺陷 — **命中 Significance + Novelty**\n\n**二、Key Insight 章节（命中 Novelty）**\n\n4. **[Key Insight / Lead Finding]** — 将 \"binary safety net breaks in multi-class\" 提升为 paper 的核心 surprising result，在 Introduction 中以 lead finding 呈现。精确化 Brenner (1993) 引用：原文说 \"in most cases the bias is in the same direction as the confounding\"，guarantee 并非完全消失而是不再可靠。将 nuance 转化为 proposal 的价值点——\"Brenner identifies the danger but cannot tell you when it strikes; our taxonomy resolves this ambiguity with exact conditions for each confusion matrix structure × DAG topology cell\" — **命中 Novelty（防止 overselling 攻击的同时锐化差异化）**\n\n5. **[Key Insight / 质变论证]** — 新增 binarization collapse 不可行论证：审稿人最自然的 trivial alternative 是\"把 K-class 折叠为 binary 再用成熟工具\"。Brenner (1993) 直接回答：binarizing polytomous variable 本身引入方向不可预测的新 bias。补充技术支撑：binary confusion matrix 2 个自由度 → scalar bias；K=5 confusion matrix 20 个自由度 → matrix-valued bias，这是结构性的复杂度跃升而非仅是参数增加 — **命中 Novelty（消除 trivial alternative 质疑）**\n\n**三、Related Work 章节（命中 Related Work）**\n\n6. **[Related Work / 致命遗漏修复]** — 新增 Lee & Wood-Doughty (arXiv:2411.03004, 2024) 引用并明确 differentiate。原始 proposal 未引用此高度相关工作。该论文将 Wood-Doughty (EMNLP 2018) 扩展至 categorical + LLM classifiers（MIMIC 临床数据），但仅覆盖 1 个 DAG topology（unobserved confounding），无 bias taxonomy，无 sensitivity analysis。将其定位为 stepping stone，修正原 proposal 中 \"Wood-Doughty and TeBlunthuis are limited to binary\" 的叙事——binary limitation 已被部分突破，但系统性多 topology 处理仍缺失 — **命中 Related Work（消除审稿人发现未引用高相关论文后直接 reject 的风险）**\n\n7. **[Related Work / 近期文献补全]** — 新增以下 2024-2026 文献（若原始 proposal 未引用）：Cheng, Mayya & Sedoc (arXiv:2412.14461) SILICON error decomposition; Dependence-Aware Label Aggregation via Ising Models (arXiv:2601.22336, AAAI 2027); CARE (arXiv:2603.00039, AAAI 2027); Multi-Perspective LLM Annotations (arXiv:2603.21404, NAACL 2027); Chew et al. (arXiv:2604.07591) annotation-as-measurement; PPI++ (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) / multiPPI++ (COLM 2024); CrossPPI\n\n_Reviewed by external pipeline (batch7), 2026-05-20_\n\n\n## MVP Validation Results\n\n- **Date**: 2026-05-20\n- **Hypothesis Supported**: Yes\n\n### Key Metrics\n\n理论vs MC一致: 5/5 (全部在95%CI内) | 非差异性C偏差范围: 0.204-0.298 (OVB=0.300) | 二分类折叠差异: Δ=0.001-0.089 | 100k随机C方向反转: 0/100000\n\n### Summary\n\nConfounding DAG + K=3 三分类标注偏差验证。推导了 OLS 中 β_T 的理论偏差闭式表达式，在 5 种混淆矩阵结构（对称高精度/非对称/结构性混淆/近随机/差异性）× 1000 MC 重复 × N=10,000 下全部验证通过。附加 100k 随机 C 矩阵搜索发现 confounding DAG 下偏差方向始终与 OVB 同向（单调），方向反转需要在 mediator/collider 等其他拓扑验证。二分类折叠对照证实多分类控制保留更多混杂信息（最大 Δ=0.089）。\n\n### Recommendation\n\nGO — 理论框架验证通过，多分类≠二分类已量化证实。方向反转（核心 claim 最强形式）需 research 阶段在 mediator/collider DAG 上验证，confounding DAG 的单调性本身是有价值的 characterization result。建议立即进入 research 阶段，优先扩展到 6 种 DAG 拓扑。\n","numLines":137,"startLine":1,"totalLines":137}}}
